# GLA transforms as code munging: transpose reverses the spine and
# dualizes each atom (copy <-> add); linear? checks a diagram uses
# only linear generators. Both are ordinary folds over reflected code.
#
# ---- what stage 9 did and did not take (2026-09-21) -----------------
# `examples/linear.braid` presents the same PROP as a THEORY, and it
# answers both of these questions STATICALLY: `transposeC` reappears
# there as `dualC`, a slot body with laws over it that `sameCodeC`
# DECIDES rather than a fold nobody audits; and `linear?` dissolves
# twice over — a def written `in Dense` can only name the theory's own
# slots, all of which are linear, and `with Dense` reads each atom of a
# base program into a slot and REFUSES by name any atom that is not a
# generator (`fmul` has no image under GLA).
#
# So why do these two stay?  Because the question they ask is not the
# one the theory answers.  A theory is a statement about code you
# WRITE, checked where you write it; these are folds over `Code`, and
# `Code` is also what `parse` and `evalCode` hand you at RUN TIME, for
# programs that did not exist when the module was checked.  A scope
# cannot be put around a string that arrives from a file.  `linear?` is
# what is left for that case, and it is the honest remainder rather
# than a duplicate: one spelling for the static question (the theory),
# one for the dynamic one (this file).
#
# The Int/Float difference is the other half of the answer.  `linear.
# braid` is over `Float` because its laws must sample at literals; this
# file dualizes `+`, which is the Int monoid.  Neither file can be the
# other at a rename.

def dualSym = (s -> (s .dup >> equals) [.+] [(s .+ >> equals) [.dup] [s] ... >> cond] ... >> cond)
def dualAtom = [(s -> s >> dualSym >> alt1 >> Atom)] [(n -> n >> alt2 >> Atom)] [(t -> t >> alt3 >> Atom)] [(y -> y >> alt4 >> Atom)] [(c -> c >> alt5 >> Atom)] [(l b -> l b >> alt6 >> Atom)] [(c -> c >> alt7 >> Atom)] ... >> foldAtom
def transposeC = reverse >> [[dualAtom] ... >> map] ... >> map

[+] >> reflect >> ((c -> [dup] (c >> transposeC) (5) >> evalAs >> print) | print) >> forget        # alt1(5, 5)
[dup >> +] >> reflect >> ((c -> [dup >> +] (c >> transposeC) (6) >> evalAs >> print) | print) >> forget  # alt1(12): x+x is symmetric

def andB = (a b -> a [b] [false] ... >> cond)
def allB = [true] [andB] ... >> foldList
def linSym = (s -> (s .dup >> equals) [true] [(s .+ >> equals) [true] [(s .swap >> equals) [true] [(s ._ >> equals) [true] [false] ... >> cond] ... >> cond] ... >> cond] ... >> cond)
def linAtom = [linSym] [drop >> true] [drop >> false] [drop >> false] [drop >> false] [drop drop >> false] [drop >> false] ... >> foldAtom
def linStage = [linAtom] ... >> map >> allB
def linear? = [linStage] ... >> map >> allB

[dup >> swap >> +] >> reflect >> (linear? >> print | print) >> forget    # alt1()
[eq? >> verdict] >> reflect >> (linear? >> print | print) >> forget      # alt2()
